    <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>

    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Question</th>
                    <th>Answer</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Why do aerosol cans leak after months of storage?</td>
                    <td>Because pressure cycling, material relaxation, and temperature exposure can gradually weaken seals even if initial testing passed.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Are all aerosol leaks visible?</td>
                    <td>No. Many leaks are microscopic and only detectable through pressure or weight-loss testing.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Is propellant choice a major leakage factor?</td>
                    <td>Yes. Propellant pressure behavior strongly influences seal stress, especially under temperature variation.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Can good crimping alone prevent leaks?</td>
                    <td>No. Crimping must work in combination with correct valve design, pressure control, and material compatibility.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Why are leakage issues more common in hot climates?</td>
                    <td>Higher ambient temperatures increase internal pressure, amplifying any existing mechanical or material weaknesses.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
